GUIDE = {
'begin': ("Many philosophical puzzles are not caused by missing facts but by words pulled out of the activities that give them sense. The cure he proposes is to return to examples and look again.", "Read him slowly and expect dialogue. A remark often stages a temptation and then examines it, so ask which voice is speaking before you underline anything.", ["At first it can sound like a refusal to answer big questions.", "Living with it, you start noticing how often a disagreement is about which use of a word is in play.", "Later still, you may see the method as a discipline of attention, and also test where it falls short."]),
'picture': ("In the Tractatus a proposition says something by being a structured model of a possible situation. Sense comes from that shared structure; truth from whether the situation obtains.", "Hold on to the structural point, not the photograph image. Ask: what are the elements, what does each stand for, and how could the arrangement be wrong?", ["At first the picture idea seems obvious, even trivial.", "Then the gap between sense and truth starts to look remarkable: you understand claims before you know if they’re true.", "Later, reading the Investigations, you may feel the pressure on the theory from greetings, orders and grief."]),
'facts': ("The world is made of how things stand, not of the things themselves. An inventory of objects leaves open every arrangement that would make a world.", "Read the numbered propositions as a tree: 2.01 and 2.04 depend on 2. Treat the English nouns cautiously and compare the German.", ["First: a neat distinction between things and facts.", "With use: you catch inventories passing as descriptions, in reports and arguments.", "Deeper: you meet the interpretive debate about what the early ontology really claims."]),
'logic': ("Logical truths are true whatever the world is like, so they carry no news; but they show the structure every meaningful proposition shares. Senseless is not nonsense.", "Keep sinnlos and unsinnig apart, and stay inside the classical setting the Tractatus assumes.", ["First: tautologies look like empty word games.", "Then: you see them as displays of logical form, and stop expecting them to inform.", "Later: you ask how far this picture survives other logics and his own later remarks on mathematics."]),
'limits': ("Some things cannot be said but show themselves, including the logical form of language. The book even suggests its own sentences are a ladder to throw away.", "Notice which reading you are bringing, traditional or resolute, and keep it separate from what 6.54 and 7 actually state.", ["First: the ending feels mystical or paradoxical.", "Then: you see the difference between a false map, a tautology and a failed representation.", "Later: you can argue for one reading while stating exactly what the other would say back."]),
'ethics': ("Ethical language reaches for an absolute value that no description of facts, however complete, could contain. He treats that reaching with respect, without turning it into a theory.", "Separate “good for something” from “good” and watch for moments when one quietly replaces the other. Remember it is a lecture, not a treatise.", ["First: it can sound like ethics is being dismissed.", "Then: you notice how often a measure replaces a moral question.", "Later: you weigh whether ethical judgements can be reasoned and even true without being reports."]),
'builders': ("Language is woven into activities. To understand words you have to see what people are doing with them: calling, bringing, warning, joking, praying.", "Take the builders as a deliberately tiny model, a lens for looking, not a full description of language.", ["First: language-games seem like a playful metaphor.", "Then: you hear conversations as moves in activities and notice mismatched replies.", "Later: you test where the model breaks — irony, power, history — and why that matters."]),
'meaning-use': ("For a large class of cases, the meaning of a word is its use in the language. Not a hidden object behind the word, but the work it does.", "Keep the qualification in §43 and remember that use includes correction, explanation and misuse.", ["First: a liberating slogan.", "Then: a practical tool for untangling words that cause friction.", "Later: you feel the hard question of what makes one use correct, and turn to rule-following."]),
'family': ("Many concepts hold together through overlapping similarities rather than one shared essence, and that is no defect. Look at cases before deciding what they must share.", "Do the exercise he asks for: list games and compare. Notice he doesn’t say definitions are never useful.", ["First: it frees you from hunting essences.", "Then: you notice how definitions are decisions with consequences.", "Later: you ask what makes a resemblance real rather than strained."]),
'life': ("Words make sense against a background of shared human activity — what he calls a form of life. Agreement in language comes before agreement in opinions.", "Read the few passages closely and treat the term as an emphasis, not a theory of culture.", ["First: a vivid way to say context matters.", "Then: a reason for patience with people trained in other practices.", "Later: the political question of who counts as “we” becomes central."]),
'going-on': ("Understanding a rule is an ability to go on correctly, sustained by training and practice, not a feeling or an inner formula that contains every future step.", "Read §185 with §§198–202. Watch for the move from “what we do” to “what is correct”.", ["First: the pupil seems merely stubborn.", "Then: you realise no finite set of examples forces a continuation.", "Later: you wrestle with whether practice can ground normativity at all."]),
'interpretation': ("If every rule needed an interpretation to apply it, understanding would never begin. Grasping a rule shows in following it, and justification eventually ends in what we do.", "Never quote the paradox of §201 without its reply; read §217 for where reasons run out.", ["First: the paradox seems devastating.", "Then: you see it as a misunderstanding Wittgenstein exposes.", "Later: you weigh skeptical and dissolving readings and form your own."]),
'private': ("A language whose words could only be understood by their speaker, fixed by inner impressions alone, would lack any standard of correctness. This does not deny inner experience.", "Keep the target exact: not diaries or secrets, but impressions that fix meaning by themselves (§243, §258).", ["First: it can sound like feelings are being denied.", "Then: you see how public our words for feelings are.", "Later: you press on whether the argument assumes its conclusion."]),
'beetle': ("If a word’s meaning depended on a private object no one else could see, the object would drop out of the word’s public use. Pain words work differently.", "Read §293 with its neighbours; notice the explicit refusal to say the box is empty.", ["First: a clever puzzle.", "Then: a corrective to “no one can know how I feel”.", "Later: you mark exactly what it leaves open about consciousness."]),
'criteria': ("Some signs don’t merely suggest a psychological state; they are part of what we mean by it, while particular judgements can still be wrong.", "Treat the criterion/evidence distinction as a first approach; interpreters disagree about its details.", ["First: a tidy distinction.", "Then: a way to challenge assessments based on the wrong signs.", "Later: a question about whose experiences our criteria miss."]),
'aspects': ("We can see the same figure differently without anything in it changing. Seeing-as sits between pure perception and interpretation.", "Cite by edition: Fragment §118 or Part II xi. Note how the remarks resist a single theory.", ["First: a fun optical puzzle.", "Then: a model of insight — seeing what was always there.", "Later: a caution that a new way of seeing is not yet a reason."]),
'hinges': ("Doubt and inquiry rest on certainties that stand fast in a practice. They are not proven, but they make proving possible, and they can shift over time.", "Read the riverbed image (§§96–99) alongside the hinges (§§341–343); interpreters disagree about what hinges are.", ["First: a reassuring image.", "Then: a tool for seeing what your checking relies on.", "Later: the hard problem of reasoning across differing hinges."]),
'doubt': ("A real doubt needs a setting: a possible error, a way to find it, and standards that survive. Some certainties stand fast rather than being known by evidence.", "Read the opening remarks against Moore and ask what the sentence “I know I have a hand” is doing in each setting.", ["First: a refutation of skepticism.", "Then: a way to proportion checking and notice idle doubt.", "Later: an open question — dissolution or evasion?"]),
'colour': ("Colour shows how a factual question and a conceptual question can share words. Some colour puzzles are about the logic of colour concepts, not physics.", "Cite part and remark together; numbering restarts in each part.", ["First: odd remarks about white and transparency.", "Then: a model for separating empirical and conceptual questions.", "Later: the difficult boundary between discovering facts and changing descriptions."]),
'will': ("Talk of laws, predictions and compulsion is often confused. A law describes regularities; it doesn’t push. Being predictable is not being forced.", "Remember these are Smythies’s notes of a lecture; cite them as mediated.", ["First: free will seems simply rescued.", "Then: you distinguish causes, reasons, coercion and prediction.", "Later: you see the traditional problem may remain, reframed."]),
'grammar': ("Philosophical grammar is the network of rules that gives an expression its use. Essence is expressed by grammar, not hidden behind it.", "Read PG §10 with PI §371–373; treat the chess comparison as this lesson’s analogy.", ["First: grammar as a broader kind of rule.", "Then: deep-sounding questions turn out to be about use.", "Later: you ask when the world should force our grammar to change."]),
'ritual': ("Rituals are not always failed science. To understand an act, ask what it expresses in a life before asking whether it works.", "This is the German Frazer article; distinguish his remarks from Rhees’s introduction.", ["First: a defence of ritual.", "Then: a habit of understanding before judging.", "Later: worry about projecting our own reactions onto others."]),
'psychology': ("Psychological words are bound up with expression and circumstance. Peacocke argues this need not settle what such words refer to.", "Attribute carefully: keep Wittgenstein’s remarks, interpretations of them, and Peacocke’s critique distinct.", ["First: feelings explained through behaviour.", "Then: a richer picture of expression and its surroundings.", "Later: the open question of reference and experience."]),
'method': ("Philosophy clarifies; it does not discover new facts. It arranges what we know so a confusion dissolves, and often requires a change of will as well as intellect.", "Read §§109–133 as a whole; note there is no single method (§133).", ["First: philosophy seems to shrink.", "Then: clarification looks like real, difficult work.", "Later: you ask, case by case, what clarification can and cannot do."]),
'reading-lab': ("Remarks are compact dialogues with qualifications. Read them as part of an investigation, never as detachable slogans.", "Use the four moves: question, paraphrase, new case, objection.", ["First: careful citation feels fussy.", "Then: you notice how often famous quotes drop a qualification.", "Later: your notes start becoming small, checkable arguments."]),
'daily-life': ("His tools sharpen attention: notice words doing too much, look at activities, and stay open to correction. They are not a complete guide to life.", "Match the tool to the problem: evidence, comparison of uses, or reasons for change.", ["First: a set of tips.", "Then: a habit of better questions.", "Later: an honest sense of what the method can’t do."]),
}
